/* ======================= TEXT PROCESSING ======================= */
lesson('awk', {
  n: 'awk', s: 'A tiny programming language for processing text line by line, split into fields (columns).',
  an: 'A spreadsheet macro that runs on every row: “if column 3 is above 70000, print columns 1 and 3” — and can keep running totals.',
  nm: 'Named after its authors <b>A</b>ho, <b>W</b>einberger and <b>K</b>ernighan (Bell Labs, 1977).',
  sx: "awk [-F SEP] [-v VAR=VAL] 'PATTERN { ACTION } ...' [FILE]...", p: [['awk', 'cmd', 'The interpreter (usually gawk or mawk).', true], ['[-F SEP]', 'opt', 'Field separator (default: runs of spaces/tabs).', false], ['[-v VAR=VAL]', 'opt', 'Pass a shell value into the program.', false], ["'PATTERN { ACTION }'", 'arg', 'The program, in single quotes so the shell leaves $1 alone. Pattern selects lines; action runs on them.', true], ['[FILE]...', 'arg', 'Input files or stdin.', false]],
  ex1: "awk -F, '$3 > 70000 {print $1, $3}' data.csv",
  sc: [['openat + read', 'reads input in chunks', 'read(3, "name,dept,salary,city\\nalice,…", 4096) = 138'], ['(per line)', 'splits the record into $1…$NF, increments NR, tests each pattern, runs matching actions', ''], ['write', 'print output (buffered)', 'write(1, "alice 95000\\ncarol 105000\\nerin 71000\\n", 37) = 37']],
  ch: 'Nothing (unless the program redirects with print > "file").', out: 'Whatever the program prints.', ec: '0, or the value given to exit, 2 on syntax errors.',
  f: [['-F SEP', '', 'Field separator (char or regex).', "awk -F: '{print $1}' /etc/passwd", true], ['-v V=X', '', 'Set a variable.', "awk -v min=70000 '$3>min' f", true], ['$0 / $1…$NF', '', 'Whole line / fields / last field.', "awk '{print $NF}' f", true], ['NR / NF', '', 'Line number / number of fields.', "awk 'NR>1' f", true], ['BEGIN / END', '', 'Run before the first / after the last line.', "awk 'END{print NR}' f", true], ['/re/', '', 'Pattern: lines matching a regex.', "awk '/ERROR/' log", false], ['printf', '', 'Formatted output.', 'awk \'{printf "%-6s %s\\n", $1, $2}\'', false], ['arr[key]++', '', 'Associative arrays for counting/grouping.', "awk '{c[$1]++} END{for(k in c) print k, c[k]}'", false]],
  e: [["awk '{print $1}' servers.txt", 'web01\nweb02\ndb01\ncache01', 'First column (space-separated).'], ["awk -F, '$3 > 70000 {print $1, $3}' data.csv", 'alice 95000\ncarol 105000\nerin 71000', 'Filter rows and pick columns.'], ["awk -F, 'NR>1 {sum+=$3} END {print \"total:\", sum, \"avg:\", sum/(NR-1)}' data.csv", 'total: 391000 avg: 78200', 'Totals and averages.'], ["awk -F, 'NR>1 {c[$2]++} END {for (d in c) print d, c[d]}' data.csv", 'eng 2\nsales 2\nops 1', 'Group and count.'], ["awk '$3 == \"down\" {print $1}' servers.txt", 'web02', 'Which servers are down.'], ["awk '{print NR\": \"$0}' fruits.txt | head -3", '1: banana\n2: apple\n3: cherry', 'Number lines.']],
  u: `<p>An awk program is a list of <b>pattern { action }</b> pairs. For every input line awk splits it into fields <code>$1 … $NF</code> (by <code>FS</code>, set with -F), then runs each action whose pattern is true. Missing pattern = every line; missing action = print the line.</p><p>Built-ins: <code>NR</code> line number, <code>NF</code> field count, <code>$0</code> whole line, <code>FS/OFS</code> input/output separators. Variables need no declaration and start as 0/"". Arrays are associative (keys are strings) — perfect for counting.</p><p><b>grep vs sed vs awk:</b> grep finds lines; sed edits lines (substitute/delete); awk understands <b>columns</b> and can compute.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>awk compiles the program to an internal bytecode, reads records separated by <code>RS</code> (newline), and lazily splits fields. Numbers vs strings: a field that looks numeric is compared numerically (“strnum”). Output is buffered (fflush() to force). gawk adds -i inplace, BEGINFILE, networking, and time functions. Classic one-liners: <code>awk '!seen[$0]++'</code> (dedupe preserving order), <code>awk 'length > 80'</code>.</p>`,
  mi: [['awk "{print $1}" file', 'Double quotes: the SHELL replaces $1 (empty) before awk sees it → prints whole lines.', "awk '{print $1}' file"], ["awk '{print $1 $2}'", 'No separator: values are concatenated.', "awk '{print $1, $2}'"], ["awk '{print $2}' data.csv", 'Default separator is whitespace, not comma.', "awk -F, '{print $2}' data.csv"]],
  safe: 'awk only reads and prints (unless the program itself writes files or calls system()).',
  r: [['cut', 'Simple column extraction without logic.'], ['sed', 'Search-and-replace editing.'], ['grep', 'Just filtering lines.'], ['sort', 'Combine: awk … | sort -k2 -n.']],
  cs: ["<code>awk '{print $1}'</code> first column", '<code>-F,</code> set separator · <code>NR</code> line · <code>NF</code> fields', "<code>'$3 > 100'</code> filter by value", "<code>'{s+=$2} END{print s}'</code> sum", 'Always single-quote the program'],
  q: [['What is $NF?', ['Number of fields', 'The last field', 'The next file', 'A syntax error'], 1, 'NF is the count; $NF the last field.'], ['Why single quotes around awk programs?', ['Style', 'So the shell doesn\'t expand $1 etc.', 'Speed', 'awk requires them'], 1, 'Double quotes let bash expand $1.'], ['Which block runs after all input?', ['BEGIN', 'END', 'FINAL', 'EXIT'], 1, 'END.']],
  c: { t: 'From <code>data.csv</code>, print the <b>name</b> and <b>salary</b> of everyone earning more than 70000.', try: "awk -F, '$3 > 70000 {print $1, $3}' data.csv", chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'awk') && /carol/.test(outOf(ctx)) && /95000/.test(outOf(ctx)) && !/bob/.test(outOf(ctx)) },
});
lesson('sed', {
  n: 'sed', s: 'A stream editor: edits text on the fly — replace, delete, insert or print specific lines.',
  an: 'A proof-reader working through a conveyor belt of lines with a rule sheet: “replace every ‘colour’ with ‘color’, delete lines starting with #”.',
  nm: '<b>s</b>tream <b>ed</b>itor.',
  sx: "sed [-n] [-E] [-i] 'SCRIPT' [FILE]...", p: [['sed', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[-n] [-E] [-i]', 'opt', '-n print only when told (p), -E extended regex, -i edit the file in place.', false], ["'SCRIPT'", 'arg', 'Commands like s/old/new/g, /re/d, 5p, optionally with addresses.', true], ['[FILE]...', 'arg', 'Input files or stdin.', false]],
  ex1: "sed 's/apple/APPLE/g' fruits.txt",
  sc: [['read', 'reads input', 'read(3, "banana\\napple\\n…", 4096) = 45'], ['(per line)', 'loads the line into the “pattern space”, applies each command whose address matches', ''], ['write', 'prints the pattern space (unless -n or d)', 'write(1, "banana\\nAPPLE\\n…", 45) = 45'], ['(-i) openat + rename', 'with -i: writes to a temp file then renames it over the original', 'rename("./sedX7a2b1", "fruits.txt") = 0']],
  ch: 'Nothing — unless -i, which rewrites the file.', out: 'The transformed text.', ec: '0, 1 invalid command, 2 file missing.',
  f: [['s/A/B/', '', 'Substitute first match of A per line.', "sed 's/a/b/' f", true], ['s/A/B/g', '', 'Substitute all matches.', "sed 's/a/b/g' f", true], ['-i', '--in-place', 'Edit the file itself (-i.bak keeps a backup).', "sed -i 's/x/y/' f", true], ['-n + p', '', 'Print only selected lines.', "sed -n '2,4p' f", true], ['/re/d', '', 'Delete matching lines.', "sed '/^#/d' f", true], ['-E', '', 'Extended regex ( ) + ? | without backslashes.', "sed -E 's/(a|b)+/X/'", false], ['N,M', '', 'Line range address; $ = last line.', "sed '1d' f", false], ['a / i / c', '', 'Append / insert / change line.', "sed '1i header' f", false], ['& / \\1', '', 'Whole match / capture group in the replacement.', "sed -E 's/(\\w+)/[\\1]/'", false]],
  e: [["sed 's/apple/APPLE/g' fruits.txt", 'banana\nAPPLE\ncherry\nAPPLE…', 'Replace all occurrences.'], ["sed -n '2,3p' notes.txt", '- everything is a file\n- the shell is a program', 'Print lines 2–3 only.'], ["sed '/INFO/d' logs/app.log | head -3", '', 'Delete INFO lines.'], ["sed '1d' data.csv", '', 'Drop the header.'], ["sed 's/,/ | /g' data.csv | head -2", 'name | dept | salary | city', 'Change separators.'], ["sed -i 's/Berlin/Munich/' data.csv; grep Munich data.csv", '', 'Edit the file in place.']],
  u: `<p>sed reads one line into the <b>pattern space</b>, runs the script on it, prints it, and moves on — so it handles files of any size. Commands can be prefixed with an <b>address</b>: a line number (<code>3</code>), range (<code>2,5</code>), last line (<code>$</code>) or regex (<code>/ERROR/</code>); <code>!</code> negates.</p><p>The substitute command <code>s/REGEX/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS</code> is 90% of real-world sed. Any delimiter works: <code>s|/usr|/opt|</code> avoids escaping slashes. In the replacement, <code>&amp;</code> = the whole match, <code>\\1</code> = first group.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>GNU sed also has a <b>hold space</b> (h/H/g/G/x) for multi-line tricks, and N/D/P for working with two lines at once. <code>-i</code> creates a new file and renames it, which means a new inode (breaks hard links, may change owner if run as root). <code>-z</code> uses NUL as line separator.</p>`,
  mi: [["sed 's/a/b/' f (expecting all replaced)", 'Only the first match on each line.', "sed 's/a/b/g' f"], ["sed -i 's/x/y/' important.conf", 'No undo.', "sed -i.bak 's/x/y/' important.conf"], ["sed 's//usr/bin//opt/bin/' f", 'Slashes clash with the delimiter.', "sed 's|/usr/bin|/opt/bin|' f"]],
  w: '<b>sed -i rewrites files in place</b> without a backup unless you give a suffix (<code>-i.bak</code>). A wrong regex can mangle every line of a config file. Run without -i first to preview, keep backups, and be extra careful with <code>sudo sed -i</code> on /etc files.',
  r: [['awk', 'Column-aware processing and calculations.'], ['grep', 'Only find lines.'], ['tr', 'Replace single characters.']],
  cs: ["<code>sed 's/old/new/g' f</code> replace all", "<code>sed -n '5,10p' f</code> print lines", "<code>sed '/re/d' f</code> delete lines", '<code>-i.bak</code> edit in place with backup', '& = match, \\1 = group'],
  q: [['What does the g in s/a/b/g mean?', ['global: all matches on the line', 'grep', 'group', 'ignore case'], 0, 'Without g only the first match per line.'], ['How to print only lines 2–4?', ["sed '2,4p'", "sed -n '2,4p'", "sed '2-4'", "sed -p 2,4"], 1, '-n suppresses default printing.'], ['Safest way to edit in place?', ['sed -i', 'sed -i.bak', 'sed > same file', 'sed -f'], 1, 'Keeps a backup.']],
  c: { t: 'Replace every “apple” with “APPLE” in the output of fruits.txt.', try: "sed 's/apple/APPLE/g' fruits.txt", chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'sed') && /APPLE/.test(outOf(ctx)) && !/apple/.test(outOf(ctx)) },
});
lesson('cut', {
  n: 'cut', s: 'Cuts out selected columns (fields) or character positions from each line.',
  an: 'Cutting specific columns out of a printed table with scissors.',
  nm: '<b>cut</b>.',
  sx: 'cut -d DELIM -f LIST [FILE]...   |   cut -c LIST [FILE]...', p: [['cut', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['-d DELIM', 'opt', 'Single-character delimiter (default TAB).', false], ['-f LIST | -c LIST', 'opt', 'Fields or character positions: 1,3 · 2-4 · 3-', true], ['[FILE]...', 'arg', 'Input files or stdin.', false]],
  ex1: 'cut -d, -f1,3 data.csv',
  sc: [['read', 'reads input', 'read(3, "name,dept,salary,city\\n…", 4096) = 138'], ['(per line)', 'splits on the delimiter and keeps the listed fields', ''], ['write', 'writes the kept fields joined by the delimiter', 'write(1, "name,salary\\nalice,95000…", 66) = 66']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'Selected columns, still separated by the delimiter.', ec: '0, 1 on error.',
  f: [['-d C', '--delimiter', 'Field delimiter (one character).', 'cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd', true], ['-f LIST', '--fields', 'Fields to keep.', 'cut -f2,4', true], ['-c LIST', '--characters', 'Character positions.', 'cut -c1-5', true], ['--complement', '', 'Keep everything EXCEPT the list.', 'cut -d, -f2 --complement', false], ['-s', '--only-delimited', 'Skip lines without the delimiter.', 'cut -s -d, -f1', false], ['--output-delimiter', '', 'Different separator in the output.', "cut -d, -f1,3 --output-delimiter=' '", false]],
  e: [['cut -d, -f1 data.csv', 'name\nalice\nbob…', 'First column.'], ['cut -d, -f1,3 data.csv', 'name,salary\nalice,95000…', 'Two columns.'], ['cut -d: -f1,7 /etc/passwd', 'root:/bin/bash…', 'User names and shells.'], ['cut -c1-10 logs/app.log | head -2', '2026-11-18\n2026-11-18', 'Characters 1–10 (the date).'], ['cut -d, -f2 data.csv | sort | uniq -c', '', 'Combine with sort/uniq.']],
  u: `<p>cut is fast and simple, but its delimiter is <b>exactly one character</b> and it doesn't understand repeated spaces — <code>cut -d' ' -f2</code> on space-aligned output (like ps) gives empty fields. Use <code>awk '{print $2}'</code> for whitespace-separated data, or <code>tr -s ' '</code> first.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>cut processes lines as bytes (GNU coreutils: -c currently equals -b for multibyte; use awk for UTF-8 columns). Fields are output in file order regardless of list order: <code>-f3,1</code> prints field 1 then 3.</p>`,
  mi: [["ps aux | cut -d' ' -f2", 'Multiple spaces → mostly empty output.', "ps aux | awk '{print $2}'"], ['cut -d, -f3,1 (expecting 3 then 1)', 'cut always keeps original order.', "awk -F, '{print $3\",\"$1}'"], ['cut -d", " -f1', '“the delimiter must be a single character”.', "awk -F', ' '{print $1}'"]],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['awk', 'Flexible fields, reordering, whitespace.'], ['tr', 'Squeeze spaces before cut.'], ['sort', 'sort -t, -k uses the same idea of fields.']],
  cs: ['<code>cut -d, -f1,3 f.csv</code>', '<code>cut -c1-10 f</code> characters', 'Delimiter: ONE character (default TAB)', 'Spaces aligned? use awk', 'Order of output = file order'],
  q: [['Default delimiter of cut?', ['space', 'comma', 'TAB', 'colon'], 2, 'TAB.'], ['Which keeps characters 1–5?', ['-f1-5', '-c1-5', '-b5', '-d5'], 1, '-c = characters.'], ['Why is cut bad for ps output?', ['Too slow', 'Columns are padded with multiple spaces', 'ps has no columns', 'It isn\'t'], 1, 'Use awk.']],
  c: { t: 'Print only the <b>name</b> and <b>city</b> columns (1 and 4) of data.csv.', try: 'cut -d, -f1,4 data.csv', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'cut') && /alice,Berlin/.test(outOf(ctx)) },
});
lesson('tr', {
  n: 'tr', s: 'Translates, squeezes or deletes individual characters from stdin.',
  an: 'A letter-swapping cipher wheel: every a becomes A, every b becomes B…',
  nm: '<b>tr</b>anslate.',
  sx: 'tr [-d] [-s] [-c] SET1 [SET2]   (reads stdin only)', p: [['tr', 'cmd', 'The command (stdin → stdout).', true], ['[-d] [-s] [-c]', 'opt', '-d delete, -s squeeze repeats, -c complement SET1.', false], ['SET1', 'arg', 'Characters to find: a-z, [:upper:], \\n…', true], ['[SET2]', 'arg', 'Replacement characters (same positions).', false]],
  ex1: "tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < fruits.txt",
  sc: [['read', 'reads stdin', 'read(0, "banana\\napple…", 8192) = 45'], ['(table lookup)', 'builds a 256-entry map once, then translates byte by byte', ''], ['write', 'writes the result', 'write(1, "BANANA\\nAPPLE…", 45) = 45']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'The transformed stream.', ec: '0, 1 on bad sets.',
  f: [['SET1 SET2', '', 'Map chars position by position.', "tr 'abc' 'xyz'", true], ['-d', '--delete', 'Delete characters in SET1.', "tr -d '0-9'", true], ['-s', '--squeeze-repeats', 'Collapse runs of the same char.', "tr -s ' '", true], ['-c', '--complement', 'Use all characters NOT in SET1.', "tr -cd 'a-z\\n'", false], ['[:class:]', '', 'upper, lower, digit, alpha, space, punct…', "tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]'", false]],
  e: [["echo hello | tr a-z A-Z", 'HELLO', 'Upper-case.'], ["tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < fruits.txt | head -2", 'BANANA\nAPPLE', 'Feed a file with <.'], ["echo 'a,b,c' | tr ',' '\\n'", 'a\nb\nc', 'Commas to newlines.'], ["echo 'phone: 555-1234' | tr -d -c '0-9\\n'", '5551234', 'Keep only digits (-c complements the set, -d deletes).'], ["echo 'too    many   spaces' | tr -s ' '", 'too many spaces', 'Squeeze spaces.']],
  u: `<p>tr works on <b>single characters</b>, not words: <code>tr 'cat' 'dog'</code> maps c→d, a→o, t→g everywhere. For words use sed. tr <b>never takes a file name</b> — feed it with &lt; or a pipe.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>tr builds a translation table of 256 bytes, so it's very fast, but it's byte-oriented: GNU tr doesn't handle multibyte UTF-8 characters (ä, é) in sets correctly. If SET2 is shorter than SET1, its last character is repeated.</p>`,
  mi: [['tr a-z A-Z fruits.txt', '“extra operand” — tr reads only stdin.', 'tr a-z A-Z < fruits.txt'], ["tr 'hello' 'world'", 'Character mapping, not word replacement: h→w, e→o, l→l, o→d.', "sed 's/hello/world/g'"], ['tr -d " \\n" to remove spaces only', 'Also removes newlines.', "tr -d ' '"]],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['sed', 'Replace words/patterns.'], ['stdin', 'tr needs < or a pipe.'], ['cut', 'Pick columns after squeezing spaces.']],
  cs: ["<code>tr a-z A-Z</code> upper-case", "<code>tr -d '\\r'</code> remove characters", "<code>tr -s ' '</code> squeeze", 'Only stdin: <code>tr … < file</code>', 'Characters, not words'],
  q: [['How does tr get its input?', ['File argument', 'stdin only', 'Both', 'Clipboard'], 1, 'Use < or a pipe.'], ["What does tr -s ' ' do?", ['Removes spaces', 'Collapses repeated spaces into one', 'Adds spaces', 'Splits lines'], 1, 'Squeeze.'], ["tr 'ab' 'xy' turns 'abba' into…", ['xyyx', 'xyba', 'abba', 'yxxy'], 0, 'a→x, b→y.']],
  c: { t: 'Convert the content of fruits.txt to UPPER CASE using tr.', try: "tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < fruits.txt", chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'tr') && /BANANA/.test(outOf(ctx)) },
});
lesson('xargs', {
  n: 'xargs', s: 'Turns lines of input into command-line ARGUMENTS for another command.',
  an: 'A secretary who takes a stack of names you hand over and fills them into a form letter, sending as many letters as needed.',
  nm: '“e<b>x</b>tended <b>args</b>” — build argument lists.',
  sx: 'COMMAND | xargs [-n N] [-I {}] [-0] [-t] COMMAND [ARGS]', p: [['xargs', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-n N per run, -I {} placeholder, -0 NUL-separated input, -t show commands, -p confirm.', false], ['COMMAND [ARGS]', 'arg', 'What to run with the collected items (default echo).', false]],
  ex1: 'echo a b c | xargs -n1 echo item',
  sc: [['read', 'reads stdin and splits on whitespace (or \\0 with -0)', 'read(0, "a b c\\n", 4096) = 6'], ['fork + execve', 'runs the command with the items appended as arguments (as many as fit / -n)', 'execve("/usr/bin/echo", ["echo", "item", "a"], …) = 0'], ['wait4', 'waits, then the next batch', 'wait4(2301, …) = 2301']],
  ch: 'Whatever the executed command does.', out: 'The output of the executed command(s).', ec: '0 all OK, 123 some command failed, 127 command not found.',
  f: [['-n N', '--max-args', 'At most N items per command.', 'xargs -n1', true], ['-I {}', '', 'Replace {} with each item (one per run).', "xargs -I {} cp {} backup/", true], ['-0', '--null', 'Items separated by NUL (pair with find -print0).', 'find . -print0 | xargs -0 rm', true], ['-t', '--verbose', 'Print each command before running.', 'xargs -t rm', false], ['-p', '--interactive', 'Ask before each command.', 'xargs -p rm', false], ['-P N', '--max-procs', 'Run N in parallel.', 'xargs -P4 -n1 gzip', false], ['-r', '--no-run-if-empty', 'Don\'t run if input is empty.', 'xargs -r rm', false]],
  e: [['echo a b c | xargs -n1 echo item', 'item a\nitem b\nitem c', 'One command per item.'], ['echo a b c | xargs echo all:', 'all: a b c', 'All items in one command.'], ['find . -name "*.txt" | xargs wc -l', '', 'Count lines of every .txt.'], ['ls docs | xargs -I {} echo "file: {}"', 'file: readme.txt…', 'Placeholder.'], ['echo notes.txt fruits.txt | xargs -t ls -l', 'ls -l notes.txt fruits.txt\n…', '-t shows the built command.']],
  u: `<p>Many commands (rm, cp, mkdir, chmod) ignore stdin — they only take <b>arguments</b>. A pipe gives text on stdin; xargs bridges the gap by reading that text and appending it to a command line.</p><p>Watch out for file names with spaces: xargs splits on whitespace. The robust pattern is <code>find … -print0 | xargs -0 cmd</code> (NUL can't appear in file names).</p>`,
  adv: `<p>xargs packs as many arguments as fit into the kernel's ARG_MAX limit (<code>getconf ARG_MAX</code>, ~2 MiB including environment), avoiding “Argument list too long” errors that <code>rm *</code> can hit with huge directories. -P runs batches concurrently — a cheap parallelism tool.</p>`,
  mi: [['find . -name "*.tmp" | xargs rm (names with spaces)', '“my file.tmp” becomes two arguments → wrong files.', 'find . -name "*.tmp" -print0 | xargs -0 rm'], ['ls | rm', 'rm ignores stdin.', 'ls | xargs rm   (or just rm *)'], ['… | xargs rm with empty input', 'GNU xargs still runs rm once (harmless error); some commands misbehave.', 'xargs -r']],
  w: '<b>xargs rm</b> deletes whatever text arrives on stdin — a wrong grep or a file name with spaces can delete unintended files. Preview with <code>xargs -t echo</code> or use <code>-p</code> to confirm; always use <code>-print0 | xargs -0</code> with find.',
  r: [['find', 'find -exec {} + does the same for find results.'], ['tee', 'Duplicate a stream instead.'], ['pipe', 'Pipes feed stdin; xargs turns it into arguments.']],
  cs: ['<code>… | xargs cmd</code> stdin → arguments', '<code>-n1</code> one per run · <code>-I {}</code> placeholder', '<code>find -print0 | xargs -0</code> safe names', '<code>-t</code> show · <code>-p</code> confirm', '<code>-P4</code> parallel'],
  q: [['Why does ls | rm not work?', ['rm needs sudo', 'rm reads arguments, not stdin', 'Pipes are broken', 'ls has no output'], 1, 'Use xargs.'], ['Which pairs safely with find -print0?', ['xargs -n0', 'xargs -0', 'xargs -z', 'xargs -s'], 1, 'NUL-separated.'], ['What does -I {} do?', ['Interactive', 'Replace {} with each item', 'Ignore errors', 'Include hidden'], 1, 'One run per item.']],
  c: { t: 'Use xargs to run <code>echo item</code> once for each of: a b c.', try: 'echo a b c | xargs -n1 echo item', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'xargs') && /item a\nitem b/.test(outOf(ctx)) },
});
lesson('tee', {
  n: 'tee', s: 'Copies its input to a file AND to stdout at the same time.',
  an: 'A T-shaped pipe fitting in plumbing: water flows on down the line and also into a bucket on the side.',
  nm: 'The plumbing <b>T</b>-splitter.',
  sx: 'COMMAND | tee [-a] FILE... [| NEXT]', p: [['tee', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[-a]', 'opt', 'Append instead of overwrite.', false], ['FILE...', 'arg', 'One or more files to write.', false]],
  ex1: 'ls | tee listing.txt',
  sc: [['openat', 'opens each file (O_TRUNC, or O_APPEND with -a)', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "listing.txt", O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0666) = 3'], ['read', 'reads a chunk from stdin', 'read(0, "backup\\nbin\\n…", 8192) = 96'], ['write ×2', 'writes the same chunk to stdout and to every file', 'write(1, …, 96) = 96; write(3, …, 96) = 96']],
  ch: 'Creates/overwrites (or appends to) the files.', out: 'Exactly its input, unchanged.', ec: '0, 1 if a file couldn\'t be written.',
  f: [['-a', '--append', 'Append to files.', 'cmd | tee -a log', true], ['FILE…', '', 'Several files at once.', 'cmd | tee a b', false], ['| sudo tee FILE', '', 'Write a root-owned file from a normal shell.', 'echo x | sudo tee /etc/f', true], ['> /dev/null', '', 'Hide the stdout copy.', 'echo x | sudo tee f > /dev/null', false]],
  e: [['ls | tee listing.txt', '', 'See AND save.'], ['echo "line" | tee -a log.txt', 'line', 'Append.'], ['sort fruits.txt | tee sorted.txt | uniq -c', '', 'Save the intermediate result in the middle of a pipeline.'], ['echo "127.0.0.1 test" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts', '', 'The correct way to append to a root file.'], ['echo hi | tee a.txt b.txt', 'hi', 'Two copies.']],
  u: `<p>tee solves two classic problems: (1) <b>logging while watching</b> — <code>./build.sh 2>&amp;1 | tee build.log</code>; (2) <b>writing root-owned files</b>: in <code>sudo echo x &gt; /etc/f</code> the redirection runs in YOUR shell and fails, but in <code>echo x | sudo tee /etc/f</code> the file is opened by tee running as root.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>tee does a simple read/write loop; with many files it writes each buffer to each fd in turn. On a broken output pipe it exits with SIGPIPE unless <code>-p</code>/<code>--output-error</code> is given. bash's process substitution allows splitting to commands: <code>cmd | tee >(gzip > a.gz) | grep x</code>.</p>`,
  mi: [['sudo echo x > /etc/f', 'Permission denied (your shell opens the file).', 'echo x | sudo tee /etc/f'], ['cmd | tee log (to keep a log)', 'Overwrites the log each run.', 'cmd | tee -a log'], ['cmd | tee log (errors missing)', 'stderr isn\'t piped.', 'cmd 2>&1 | tee log']],
  w: 'Without <code>-a</code>, tee <b>overwrites</b> its files — <code>| sudo tee /etc/hosts</code> instead of <code>tee -a</code> replaces your whole hosts file with one line.',
  r: [['redir', '> writes only to the file, not the screen.'], ['append', '>> vs tee -a.'], ['xargs', 'Turn input into arguments instead.']],
  cs: ['<code>cmd | tee file</code> screen + file', '<code>tee -a</code> append', '<code>| sudo tee /etc/x</code> write root files', '<code>2>&1 | tee log</code> include errors', 'Overwrites without -a!'],
  q: [['What does tee output on stdout?', ['Nothing', 'Its input unchanged', 'The file name', 'Errors'], 1, 'Pass-through.'], ['Why does echo x | sudo tee /etc/f work but sudo echo x > /etc/f not?', ['tee is magic', 'tee (running as root) opens the file', 'echo is blocked', 'Bash bug'], 1, 'The redirection is done by your shell.'], ['Which appends?', ['tee -p', 'tee -a', 'tee >>', 'tee -i'], 1, '-a.']],
  c: { t: 'Show the output of <code>ls</code> on screen AND save it to <code>files.txt</code> in one go.', try: 'ls | tee files.txt', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'tee') && has('files.txt') && (HP('files.txt').n.data || '').includes('docs') },
});

/* ======================= PERMISSIONS DEEP DIVE ======================= */
lesson('chmod-octal', {
  n: 'chmod octal', k: 'chmod', s: 'Writing permissions as numbers: each digit = r(4) + w(2) + x(1), with an optional 4th digit for special bits.',
  an: 'A combination lock with three dials (owner, group, others); each dial can show 0–7, and the number encodes which of read/write/execute are open.',
  nm: '<b>Octal</b> = base 8: one digit (0–7) holds exactly 3 bits.',
  sx: 'chmod [SPECIAL]OWNER GROUP OTHERS FILE   e.g. chmod 0755 file', p: [['chmod', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[SPECIAL]', 'arg', 'Optional first digit: 4 setuid, 2 setgid, 1 sticky.', false], ['OWNER GROUP OTHERS', 'arg', 'Three digits 0–7.', true], ['FILE', 'arg', 'Target(s).', true]],
  ex1: 'chmod 750 script.sh',
  sc: [['fchmodat', 'sets the mode bits exactly to the given number', 'fchmodat(AT_FDCWD, "script.sh", 0750) = 0']],
  ch: 'The mode bits in the inode.', out: 'Nothing.', ec: '0 / 1.',
  f: [['7 = rwx', '', '4+2+1', 'chmod 700 private', true], ['6 = rw-', '', '4+2', 'chmod 600 key', true], ['5 = r-x', '', '4+1', 'chmod 755 dir', true], ['4 = r--', '', 'read only', 'chmod 644 file', true], ['0 = ---', '', 'nothing', 'chmod 750', false], ['4xxx', '', 'setuid', 'chmod 4755 prog', false], ['2xxx', '', 'setgid', 'chmod 2775 shared', false], ['1xxx', '', 'sticky', 'chmod 1777 /tmp', false]],
  e: [['chmod 644 notes.txt; ls -l notes.txt', '-rw-r--r-- …', 'Typical file.'], ['chmod 755 script.sh; ls -l script.sh', '-rwxr-xr-x …', 'Typical program / directory.'], ['chmod 600 data.csv; ls -l data.csv', '-rw------- …', 'Private.'], ['chmod 750 projects; ls -ld projects', 'drwxr-x--- …', 'Team-only directory.'], ['stat -c "%a %A %n" notes.txt script.sh', '644 -rw-r--r-- notes.txt…', 'Show octal and symbolic side by side.']],
  u: `<p>Each permission set is 3 bits, so it fits one octal digit: <b>r = 4, w = 2, x = 1</b>. Add them: rwx = 7, rw- = 6, r-x = 5, r-- = 4, -wx = 3, -w- = 2, --x = 1. Three digits = owner, group, others.</p><div data-calc="750"></div><p>Common values: <b>644</b> files, <b>755</b> dirs & programs, <b>600</b> secrets (SSH keys), <b>700</b> private dirs, <b>775/664</b> shared team folders, <b>777</b> almost never.</p><p>Octal SETS all bits at once; symbolic (<code>u+x</code>) CHANGES only some. Use symbolic when you don't want to touch the other bits.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>The full mode is 16 bits: 4 type bits (S_IFREG 0100000, S_IFDIR 0040000…) + 3 special (S_ISUID 04000, S_ISGID 02000, S_ISVTX 01000) + 9 permission bits. <code>stat -c %a</code> prints the low 12 in octal; <code>%f</code> prints the raw hex mode.</p>`,
  mi: [['chmod 755 file.txt', 'Text files don\'t need x.', 'chmod 644 file.txt'], ['chmod 640 (thinking 6 = r and x)', '6 = rw-.', 'r-x = 5'], ['chmod 777 to “fix” permission errors', 'Opens the file to every user.', 'Find out which user needs which bit']],
  w: 'Octal mode <b>replaces all bits</b> — a wrong digit can expose private files (e.g. 644 on an SSH private key) or lock everyone out. Combined with <code>-R</code> it affects whole trees.',
  r: [['chmod', 'Symbolic mode (u+x).'], ['setuid', 'The 4th digit explained.'], ['umask', 'Default modes for new files.']],
  cs: ['r=4 w=2 x=1 → add per digit', '644 file · 755 dir/program · 600 secret', '4th digit: 4 setuid · 2 setgid · 1 sticky', 'Octal sets everything at once', '<code>stat -c %a f</code> shows octal'],
  q: [['What is rwxr-x--- in octal?', ['750', '754', '640', '751'], 0, '7 5 0.'], ['Octal for rw-r--r--?', ['755', '644', '664', '600'], 1, '6 4 4.'], ['What does the 1 in 1777 mean?', ['setuid', 'setgid', 'sticky bit', 'nothing'], 2, 'Sticky (as on /tmp).']],
  c: { t: 'Give <code>data.csv</code> mode <b>640</b> (owner rw, group r, others nothing).', try: 'chmod 640 data.csv', chk: ctx => { const r = HP('data.csv'); return r.n && (r.n.mode & 0o777) === 0o640; } },
});
lesson('umask', {
  n: 'umask', s: 'Sets which permission bits are REMOVED from newly created files and directories.',
  an: 'A stencil laid over every new file: the holes it covers are always blanked out, so new files never start with more permission than you allow.',
  nm: '<b>u</b>ser file-creation <b>mask</b>.',
  sx: 'umask [-S] [MASK]', p: [['umask', 'cmd', 'Shell builtin (per process, inherited).', true], ['[-S]', 'opt', 'Show symbolically (u=rwx,g=rx,o=rx).', false], ['[MASK]', 'arg', 'New mask in octal, e.g. 022, 027, 077.', false]],
  ex1: 'umask 077',
  sc: [['umask', 'sets the process\'s mask; returns the old one', 'umask(077) = 022'], ['(later) openat/mkdir', 'the kernel applies mode & ~umask to every new file', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "secret", O_CREAT|…, 0666) → mode 0600']],
  ch: 'Only the shell\'s mask (and children started afterwards).', out: 'Without argument: the current mask, e.g. 0022.', ec: '0, 1 on invalid mask.',
  f: [['(none)', '', 'Print the mask.', 'umask', true], ['-S', '', 'Symbolic form of the resulting permissions.', 'umask -S', true], ['022', '', 'Default: files 644, dirs 755.', 'umask 022', true], ['027', '', 'Group read, others nothing: 640 / 750.', 'umask 027', false], ['077', '', 'Private: 600 / 700.', 'umask 077', true]],
  e: [['umask', '0022', 'Current mask.'], ['umask -S', 'u=rwx,g=rx,o=rx', 'Resulting dir permissions.'], ['umask 077; touch secret; ls -l secret', '-rw------- … secret', 'New files are private.'], ['mkdir privdir; ls -ld privdir', 'drwx------ …', 'Dirs too.'], ['umask 022', '', 'Back to normal (or put it in ~/.bashrc).']],
  u: `<p>Programs request a mode when creating files — usually <b>666</b> for files and <b>777</b> for directories. The kernel then clears every bit that is set in the umask: <code>mode &amp; ~umask</code>. With umask 022: 666 → 644, 777 → 755. With 077: 600 / 700.</p><p>It's not subtraction but “switch off these bits”: 666 with umask 033 gives 644, not 633. x on files is never added by the umask — files only become executable via chmod.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>umask lives in <code>current->fs->umask</code>, is inherited at fork, and applied in <code>vfs_create/vfs_mkdir</code> unless the directory has a default ACL (then the ACL wins). System default comes from <code>/etc/login.defs</code> (UMASK) or pam_umask; systemd services set <code>UMask=</code> in unit files.</p>`,
  mi: [['umask 777 to make files executable', 'umask REMOVES bits — 777 gives files with no permissions at all.', 'chmod +x file'], ['Setting umask in a terminal and expecting it everywhere', 'Only that shell and its children.', 'Put it in ~/.bashrc / ~/.profile'], ['umask 644', 'Treated as a mask: removes rw for owner…', 'umask 022 (mask, not mode)']],
  safe: 'Affects only files created later in this shell.',
  r: [['chmod', 'Change existing files.'], ['chmod-octal', 'Octal math.'], ['bashrc', 'Make it permanent.']],
  cs: ['New file = 666 & ~umask · dir = 777 & ~umask', '022 → 644/755 (default)', '077 → 600/700 (private)', '<code>umask -S</code> symbolic', 'Per shell; put in ~/.bashrc'],
  q: [['With umask 027, a new file gets…', ['640', '750', '666', '027'], 0, '666 & ~027 = 640.'], ['Does umask change existing files?', ['Yes', 'No', 'Only with -R', 'Only dirs'], 1, 'Only future files.'], ['umask 077 makes new directories…', ['777', '755', '700', '000'], 2, '777 & ~077 = 700.']],
  c: { t: 'Set your umask so new files are private (<b>077</b>), then create <code>secret.txt</code> and check it is <code>-rw-------</code>.', try: 'umask 077', chk: ctx => { const r = HP('secret.txt'); return r.n && (r.n.mode & 0o777) === 0o600; } },
});
lesson('setuid', {
  n: 'setuid / setgid', k: 'chmod', s: 'Special permission bits that make a program run with its OWNER\'s (or group\'s) privileges, and make directories pass their group on.',
  an: 'A vending machine that is allowed into the bank vault on your behalf: you press the button, the machine (owned by the bank) does the privileged part — only the specific job it was built for.',
  nm: '<b>set</b> <b>u</b>ser <b>ID</b> / <b>set</b> <b>g</b>roup <b>ID</b> (on execution).',
  sx: 'chmod u+s FILE  |  chmod g+s FILE_OR_DIR  |  chmod 4755 / 2755', p: [['chmod', 'cmd', 'Set the bits with chmod.', true], ['u+s / 4xxx', 'arg', 'setuid: run with the file owner\'s UID.', false], ['g+s / 2xxx', 'arg', 'setgid: run with the file\'s group; on a DIRECTORY new files inherit its group.', false]],
  ex1: 'ls -l /usr/bin/passwd',
  sc: [['execve', 'when executing a setuid file, the kernel sets the process\'s EFFECTIVE UID to the file owner', 'execve("/usr/bin/passwd", …) = 0   → euid 0, ruid 1000'], ['(inside)', 'the program can now do the privileged work (edit /etc/shadow) and must carefully check what the real user asked for', '']],
  ch: 'chmod u+s/g+s changes mode bits; at run time the process gets different credentials.', out: 'ls -l shows s (or S if x is missing) in the owner/group execute slot.', ec: '—',
  f: [['u+s / 4000', '', 'setuid.', 'chmod u+s prog', true], ['g+s / 2000', '', 'setgid (files and dirs).', 'chmod g+s shared/', true], ['s vs S', '', 's = bit + x set; S = bit set but no x (useless).', 'ls -l', false], ['find -perm -4000', '', 'List setuid files (security audit).', 'find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null', true], ['nosuid', '', 'Mount option ignoring these bits.', 'mount -o nosuid', false]],
  e: [['ls -l /usr/bin/passwd /usr/bin/sudo', '-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root … /usr/bin/passwd\n-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root … /usr/bin/sudo', 'The s: runs as root.'], ['ls -l /usr/bin/crontab', '-rwxr-sr-x 1 root 107 …', 'setgid: runs with the crontab group.'], ['find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null', '', 'Audit all setuid programs.'], ['sudo mkdir /srv/team; sudo chgrp developers /srv/team; sudo chmod 2775 /srv/team', '', 'Shared folder: new files get group developers.'], ['ls -ld /srv/team', 'drwxrwsr-x …', 'The s in the group slot.']],
  u: `<p>Normally a program runs with the UID of whoever started it. With <b>setuid</b>, the kernel runs it with the UID of the file's <b>owner</b>. That's how <code>passwd</code> (owned by root) can update the root-only /etc/shadow when you change your password, and how <code>sudo</code> works.</p><p><b>setgid on a directory</b> is the useful everyday feature: files created inside inherit the directory's group instead of the creator's primary group — perfect for team folders.</p><p>Every setuid-root program is a potential privilege-escalation hole: a bug in it lets attackers become root. Keep the list short (<code>find / -perm -4000</code>) and never set it on scripts.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Processes have real, effective and saved UIDs. At execve of a setuid file, euid = suid = file owner; the program can drop privileges with <code>setresuid()</code>. Linux ignores setuid on interpreted scripts (#! files) because of race conditions. File capabilities (<code>setcap cap_net_raw+ep /usr/bin/ping</code>) are the fine-grained modern alternative; mounts with <code>nosuid</code> (e.g. /tmp, USB) ignore the bits.</p>`,
  mi: [['chmod u+s myscript.sh', 'Linux ignores setuid on scripts.', 'Use sudo rules instead'], ['chmod 4777 something', 'Anyone can modify a program that runs as root → instant root.', 'Never combine setuid with world-writable'], ['Expecting g+s on a dir to change existing files', 'Only NEW files inherit the group.', 'chgrp -R team dir once, then g+s']],
  w: 'A <b>setuid-root</b> binary that is buggy or writable by others gives attackers full root access. Don\'t add setuid to programs yourself; audit with <code>find / -perm -4000</code>; mount removable media and /tmp with <code>nosuid</code>.',
  r: [['sudo', 'The safe, configurable way to run things as root.'], ['sticky', 'The third special bit.'], ['chmod-octal', '4/2/1 in the first digit.']],
  cs: ['setuid (u+s, 4xxx): run as file OWNER', 'setgid (g+s, 2xxx): run as group / dirs pass group on', 'ls -l shows <b>s</b> in the x slot', 'Audit: <code>find / -perm -4000</code>', 'Ignored on scripts and nosuid mounts'],
  q: [['Why is /usr/bin/passwd setuid root?', ['Speed', 'It must write root-only /etc/shadow for normal users', 'History', 'It isn\'t'], 1, 'Classic use case.'], ['What does setgid on a directory do?', ['Nothing', 'New files inherit the directory\'s group', 'Only root can enter', 'Files become executable'], 1, 'Great for shared folders.'], ['S (capital) in ls -l means…', ['super', 'bit set but x missing', 'sticky', 'socket'], 1, 'Usually a mistake.']],
  c: { t: 'Find all <b>setuid</b> programs on the system (hide the permission errors).', try: 'find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'find') && ctx.line.includes('4000') && outOf(ctx).includes('/usr/bin/passwd') },
});
lesson('sticky', {
  n: 'sticky bit', k: 'chmod', s: 'On a directory: everyone may create files, but only a file\'s owner (or the dir owner/root) may delete or rename it.',
  an: 'A shared fridge in an office: anyone can put food in, but you may only take out your own labelled food.',
  nm: 'Historically it made programs “stick” in memory; today it means “restricted deletion” on directories.',
  sx: 'chmod +t DIR   |   chmod 1777 DIR', p: [['chmod', 'cmd', 'Set with chmod.', true], ['+t / 1xxx', 'arg', 'The sticky bit.', true], ['DIR', 'arg', 'A (usually world-writable) directory.', true]],
  ex1: 'ls -ld /tmp',
  sc: [['unlinkat → EPERM', 'in a sticky dir, unlink/rename checks: caller owns the file, or owns the dir, or is root', 'unlinkat(AT_FDCWD, "/tmp/alice.txt", 0) = -1 EPERM (Operation not permitted)']],
  ch: 'The t bit on the directory.', out: 'ls -ld shows t (or T without x for others) at the end: drwxrwxrwt.', ec: '—',
  f: [['+t', '', 'Set sticky.', 'chmod +t shared', true], ['1777', '', 'Classic /tmp mode.', 'chmod 1777 dir', true], ['-t', '', 'Remove.', 'chmod -t dir', false]],
  e: [['ls -ld /tmp', 'drwxrwxrwt 2 root root 4096 … /tmp', 'The t at the end.'], ['mkdir drop; chmod 1777 drop; ls -ld drop', 'drwxrwxrwt …', 'Create a shared drop folder.'], ['touch /tmp/mine.txt; ls -l /tmp', '', 'You can create files in /tmp.'], ['sudo -u alice rm /tmp/mine.txt', 'rm: cannot remove …: Operation not permitted', 'alice can\'t delete your file there.'], ['stat -c %a /tmp', '1777', 'Octal with the special digit.']],
  u: `<p>Normally, <b>write permission on a directory</b> lets you delete ANY file in it — even files owned by others (deleting edits the directory, not the file). That would be chaos in /tmp, where everyone can write. The sticky bit adds the rule: only the file's owner, the directory's owner, or root may delete/rename.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Checked in <code>may_delete()</code> → <code>check_sticky()</code>. Since Linux 4.19, <code>fs.protected_regular</code> / <code>protected_fifos</code> additionally stop O_CREAT opens of files owned by others in sticky world-writable dirs (defence against /tmp races); <code>protected_symlinks</code> blocks following others' symlinks there.</p>`,
  mi: [['chmod 777 shared (without t)', 'Any user can delete anyone\'s files.', 'chmod 1777 shared'], ['Setting +t on a file', 'Ignored on Linux.', 'Only meaningful on directories'], ['Assuming sticky protects file CONTENT', 'Others can still write to files if the file mode allows.', 'Set file permissions correctly too']],
  safe: 'Adding the sticky bit only restricts deletion.',
  r: [['setuid', 'The other special bits.'], ['chmod-octal', 'The 1 in 1777.'], ['rm', 'Deletion needs w on the directory.']],
  cs: ['Sticky dir: only owners delete their files', '<code>chmod +t dir</code> / <code>1777</code>', '/tmp is drwxrwxrwt', 'Deleting = writing to the directory', 'No effect on plain files'],
  q: [['Which directory classically has the sticky bit?', ['/home', '/tmp', '/etc', '/bin'], 1, 'drwxrwxrwt.'], ['Without the sticky bit, a user with w on a dir can…', ['only add files', 'delete any file in it', 'nothing', 'read others\' files'], 1, 'Deleting edits the directory.'], ['Octal for rwxrwxrwx + sticky?', ['0777', '1777', '2777', '4777'], 1, '1 = sticky.']],
  c: { t: 'Create a folder <code>drop</code> that everyone can write into but nobody can delete others\' files from.', try: 'mkdir drop; chmod 1777 drop', chk: ctx => { const r = HP('drop'); return r.n && r.n.t === 'd' && (r.n.mode & 0o1000) && (r.n.mode & 0o002); } },
});
lesson('acl', {
  n: 'getfacl / setfacl', k: 'setfacl', s: 'Access Control Lists: grant permissions to specific extra users or groups beyond owner/group/others.',
  an: 'A guest list on top of the normal house keys: “Alice may also come in and use the kitchen”, without giving her the family key.',
  nm: '<b>get</b>/<b>set</b> <b>f</b>ile <b>ACL</b> (Access Control List).',
  sx: 'setfacl -m u:USER:PERMS FILE  |  setfacl -x u:USER FILE  |  getfacl FILE', p: [['setfacl / getfacl', 'cmd', 'Modify / show ACLs.', true], ['-m / -x / -b', 'opt', 'modify, remove entry, remove all.', true], ['u:USER:rwx / g:GROUP:rx', 'arg', 'The ACL entry.', true], ['FILE', 'arg', 'Target(s).', true]],
  ex1: 'setfacl -m u:alice:rw notes.txt',
  sc: [['setxattr', 'stores the ACL as an extended attribute of the inode', 'setxattr("notes.txt", "system.posix_acl_access", …, 44, 0) = 0'], ['getxattr', 'getfacl reads it back', 'getxattr("notes.txt", "system.posix_acl_access", …) = 44']],
  ch: 'Extended attributes of the inode; ls -l shows a + after the mode.', out: 'getfacl: # file, # owner, user::, user:alice:, group::, mask::, other::', ec: '0 / 1.',
  f: [['-m ENTRY', '', 'Add/modify an entry.', 'setfacl -m u:alice:r f', true], ['-x ENTRY', '', 'Remove an entry.', 'setfacl -x u:alice f', true], ['-b', '', 'Remove all ACLs.', 'setfacl -b f', false], ['-R', '', 'Recursive.', 'setfacl -R -m g:dev:rx dir', false], ['-d', '', 'Default ACL on a directory (inherited by new files).', 'setfacl -d -m g:dev:rw dir', false]],
  e: [['setfacl -m u:alice:rw notes.txt', '', 'Alice may read+write.'], ['getfacl notes.txt', '# file: notes.txt\n# owner: user\nuser::rw-\nuser:alice:rw-\ngroup::r--\nmask::rw-\nother::r--', 'Show all entries.'], ['ls -l notes.txt', '-rw-r--r--+ 1 user user …', 'Note the +.'], ['setfacl -x u:alice notes.txt', '', 'Remove alice\'s entry.'], ['setfacl -m g:developers:r data.csv', '', 'A whole group.']],
  u: `<p>The classic model has one owner and one group. ACLs let you add entries like <code>user:alice:rw-</code> or <code>group:auditors:r--</code>. The <b>mask</b> entry limits the maximum permission for all named entries and the group. <b>Default ACLs</b> on directories are inherited by new files — a powerful way to manage shared project folders.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>POSIX ACLs are stored in the <code>system.posix_acl_access</code> / <code>system.posix_acl_default</code> xattrs. The kernel evaluates: owner → named users → (group + named groups, limited by mask) → other. With an ACL present, <code>chmod g=…</code> actually sets the mask. Filesystems need ACL support (ext4/xfs/btrfs: on by default).</p>`,
  mi: [['Forgetting the mask', 'Named entries can be silently limited (“#effective:r--”).', 'setfacl -m m::rwx f'], ['cp without -a loses ACLs', 'The copy has none.', 'cp -a / rsync -A'], ['Using ACLs everywhere', 'Hard to audit.', 'Prefer groups; use ACLs for exceptions']],
  safe: 'Grants or removes extra access — review who gets what.',
  r: [['chmod', 'Classic permissions.'], ['groups', 'Often a group is simpler than an ACL.'], ['chown', 'Owner/group.']],
  cs: ['<code>setfacl -m u:alice:rw f</code>', '<code>getfacl f</code> show', '<code>setfacl -x u:alice f</code> remove', 'ls -l shows + when ACLs exist', 'Default ACLs (-d) for inheritance'],
  q: [['What does the + in -rw-r--r--+ mean?', ['setuid', 'The file has ACL entries', 'Hidden file', 'Executable'], 1, 'Extended permissions.'], ['Which grants alice read/write?', ['setfacl -m u:alice:rw f', 'chmod alice+rw f', 'chown alice f', 'getfacl -a alice f'], 0, 'u:USER:PERMS.'], ['Where are ACLs stored?', ['/etc/acl', 'Extended attributes of the inode', 'In the file content', '~/.acl'], 1, 'xattrs.']],
  c: { t: 'Give <b>alice</b> read+write access to <code>fruits.txt</code> via an ACL, then display the ACL.', try: 'setfacl -m u:alice:rw fruits.txt', steps: [['Set the ACL entry', ctx => { const r = HP('fruits.txt'); return r.n && r.n.acl && r.n.acl['u:alice'] === 'rw-'; }], ['Show it with getfacl', ctx => okRun(ctx, 'getfacl')]] },
});

/* ======================= PROCESS INTERNALS ======================= */
lesson('strace', {
  n: 'strace', s: 'Shows every system call a program makes (and their results) — X-ray vision for processes.',
  an: 'Wiretapping the phone line between a program and the kernel: you hear every request (“open this file”) and every answer (“here’s fd 3” / “no such file”).',
  nm: '<b>s</b>ystem-call <b>trace</b>.',
  sx: 'strace [-c] [-e trace=SET] [-f] [-p PID | COMMAND [ARGS]]', p: [['strace', 'cmd', 'The tracer.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-c summary, -e filter, -f follow children, -o file, -T times.', false], ['-p PID | COMMAND', 'arg', 'Attach to a running process or start a new one.', true]],
  ex1: 'strace cat notes.txt',
  sc: [['ptrace(PTRACE_SEIZE)', 'strace becomes the tracer of the target', 'ptrace(PTRACE_SEIZE, 2401, NULL, PTRACE_O_TRACESYSGOOD) = 0'], ['(stop at every syscall)', 'the kernel stops the target on entry and exit of each syscall; strace reads registers to decode name, args and result', 'ptrace(PTRACE_GETREGSET, 2401, …)'], ['write(2, …)', 'strace prints each decoded call to stderr', 'write(2, "openat(AT_FDCWD, \\"notes.txt\\"…", 60) = 60']],
  ch: 'Nothing (the traced program runs normally, just slower).', out: 'One line per syscall on <b>stderr</b>: <code>name(args) = result</code>; errors show <code>-1 ENOENT (No such file or directory)</code>. The program\'s own output goes to stdout as usual.', ec: 'The traced program\'s exit code.',
  f: [['-c', '', 'Summary table: counts, errors, time per syscall.', 'strace -c ls', true], ['-e trace=SET', '', 'Only some calls: openat, file, network, process.', 'strace -e trace=openat cat f', true], ['-p PID', '', 'Attach to a running process (needs permission).', 'sudo strace -p 520', true], ['-f', '', 'Follow forked children.', 'strace -f bash script.sh', false], ['-o FILE', '', 'Write the trace to a file.', 'strace -o t.txt cmd', false], ['-T / -t', '', 'Time spent in each call / timestamps.', 'strace -T cmd', false], ['-s N', '', 'Show N chars of strings.', 'strace -s 200 cmd', false]],
  e: [['strace cat notes.txt', 'execve("/usr/bin/cat", ["cat", "notes.txt"], …) = 0\n…\nopenat(AT_FDCWD, "notes.txt", O_RDONLY) = 3\nread(3, "Linux notes\\n- "…, 131072) = 104', 'Every step of cat.'], ['strace cat missing.txt', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "missing.txt", O_RDONLY) = -1 ENOENT (No such file or directory)', 'See exactly why something fails.'], ['strace -c ls', '% time  seconds  usecs/call  calls  errors syscall…', 'Statistics.'], ['strace -e trace=openat ls', '', 'Only file opens — which config files does a program read?'], ['strace -p 888', '', 'Attach to a running process you own.']],
  u: `<p>Programs can't touch hardware or other processes directly; everything goes through <b>system calls</b> into the kernel. strace makes them visible, which answers questions like “which config file is it reading?”, “why permission denied?”, “why is it hanging?” (stuck in <code>read</code>/<code>futex</code>/<code>connect</code>).</p><p>Reading the output: <code>openat(AT_FDCWD, "notes.txt", O_RDONLY) = 3</code> → opened, got file descriptor 3. <code>= -1 EACCES</code> → failed with an errno. The first lines are always the dynamic loader opening shared libraries (libc).</p>`,
  adv: `<p>strace uses <code>ptrace(2)</code> with PTRACE_SYSCALL (or seccomp-bpf filtering with --seccomp-bpf for lower overhead). Each traced syscall costs two context switches → programs can be 10–100× slower. Attaching to others' processes requires CAP_SYS_PTRACE and is limited by <code>/proc/sys/kernel/yama/ptrace_scope</code>. For production, eBPF tools (bpftrace, perf trace) have far lower overhead.</p>`,
  mi: [['strace cmd | grep open', 'strace writes to STDERR — grep sees only the program\'s output.', 'strace cmd 2>&1 | grep open   (or -o file)'], ['strace -p 1 as a normal user', 'Operation not permitted.', 'sudo strace -p PID'], ['strace on a busy production server', 'Slows the process dramatically.', 'Use -c briefly, or perf/bpftrace']],
  w: 'strace can <b>slow a traced process massively</b> and exposes everything it reads and writes — including passwords and secrets. Use it on production services only briefly and with care.',
  r: [['lsof', 'Which files are open right NOW (snapshot), not the call history.'], ['proc', '/proc/PID/ shows a process\'s current state.'], ['ps', 'Find the PID to attach to.']],
  cs: ['<code>strace cmd</code> trace all syscalls', '<code>-e trace=openat</code> filter', '<code>-c</code> statistics · <code>-f</code> children', '<code>-p PID</code> attach', 'Output on stderr: use 2>&1'],
  q: [['Where does strace print its trace?', ['stdout', 'stderr', 'a log file', 'syslog'], 1, 'Use 2>&1 or -o.'], ['What does "= -1 ENOENT" mean?', ['Success', 'The call failed: no such file or directory', 'Permission denied', 'Timeout'], 1, 'errno ENOENT.'], ['Which kernel mechanism does strace use?', ['inotify', 'ptrace', 'cgroups', 'netlink'], 1, 'ptrace.']],
  c: { t: 'Trace the system calls of <code>cat notes.txt</code> (or any file) and find the <code>openat</code> call.', try: 'strace cat fruits.txt', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'strace') && ctx.events.some(e => e.type === 'strace') },
});
lesson('lsof', {
  n: 'lsof', s: 'Lists open files — including network sockets, pipes and devices — and which process holds them.',
  an: 'Asking the library desk: “who has checked out which books right now?” — for files, ports and connections.',
  nm: '<b>l</b>i<b>s</b>t <b>o</b>pen <b>f</b>iles.',
  sx: 'lsof [-p PID] [-u USER] [-i [:PORT]] [FILE]', p: [['lsof', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[-p PID] [-u USER]', 'opt', 'Filter by process or user.', false], ['[-i [:PORT]]', 'opt', 'Network sockets (optionally one port).', false], ['[FILE]', 'arg', 'Who has this file open?', false]],
  ex1: 'lsof -p 888',
  sc: [['readlink /proc/PID/fd/*', 'each fd entry is a symlink to the open file/socket/pipe', 'readlinkat(AT_FDCWD, "/proc/888/fd/3", "socket:[48213]", 4096) = 14'], ['read /proc/net/tcp', 'maps socket inodes to addresses/ports', 'read(5, "  sl  local_address rem_address …", 4096) = 900'], ['write', 'prints the table', 'write(1, "COMMAND PID USER FD TYPE …", 512) = 512']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'COMMAND PID USER FD TYPE DEVICE SIZE/OFF NODE NAME.', ec: '0 = found something, 1 = nothing matched.',
  f: [['-p PID', '', 'One process.', 'lsof -p 888', true], ['-i', '', 'Network connections.', 'sudo lsof -i', true], ['-i :PORT', '', 'Who uses a port?', 'sudo lsof -i :80', true], ['-u USER', '', 'One user.', 'lsof -u user', false], ['FILE / +D DIR', '', 'Who has this file / anything under DIR open?', 'lsof /var/log/syslog', true], ['-t', '', 'Only PIDs (for scripts).', 'kill $(lsof -t -i :8000)', false], ['+L1', '', 'Deleted-but-open files (space not freed!).', 'sudo lsof +L1', false]],
  e: [['lsof -p 888', 'python3 888 user 3u IPv4 … TCP *:8000 (LISTEN)\npython3 888 user 4u REG … /home/user/logs/app.log', 'Everything one process has open.'], ['lsof -i :8000', '', 'Which process listens on port 8000.'], ['sudo lsof -i :80', '', 'Port 80 belongs to nginx (root\'s process, needs sudo).'], ['lsof -u user', '', 'All files of a user.'], ['lsof -t -i :8000', '888', 'Just the PID — combine with kill.']],
  u: `<p>“Everything is a file”: regular files, directories, terminals, pipes and network sockets are all <b>file descriptors</b>. lsof lists them by reading <code>/proc/PID/fd/</code>. The <b>FD</b> column: numbers with r/w/u (read/write/both), plus <code>cwd</code> (working dir), <code>txt</code> (program binary), <code>mem</code> (mapped libraries).</p><p>Typical uses: “port already in use” (who has :8080?), “device is busy” on umount (who has files open there?), “disk full but du disagrees” (deleted files still open: <code>lsof +L1</code>).</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Each <code>/proc/PID/fd/N</code> is a magic symlink to the <code>struct file</code> in the process's fd table (<code>files_struct</code>); sockets show as <code>socket:[inode]</code>, matched against <code>/proc/net/tcp|udp|unix</code>. <code>/proc/PID/fdinfo/N</code> shows position and flags. Non-root users can only inspect their own processes.</p>`,
  mi: [['lsof -i :80 as normal user (empty)', 'Other users\' sockets are hidden.', 'sudo lsof -i :80'], ['lsof without filters', 'Thousands of lines.', 'Always filter: -p, -i, -u, or a path'], ['Deleting a log that\'s held open', 'Space is not freed until the process closes it.', 'Truncate instead: > logfile, or restart the service']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['ss', 'Faster for sockets only.'], ['strace', 'Watch file operations as they happen.'], ['proc', '/proc/PID/fd is the raw source.'], ['umount', 'lsof explains “target is busy”.']],
  cs: ['<code>lsof -p PID</code> files of a process', '<code>lsof -i :8080</code> who uses a port', '<code>lsof /path</code> who has a file open', '<code>-t</code> PIDs only', '<code>lsof +L1</code> deleted but open'],
  q: [['Which shows who listens on port 8000?', ['lsof -p 8000', 'lsof -i :8000', 'lsof 8000', 'lsof -u 8000'], 1, '-i :PORT.'], ['FD “cwd” means…', ['file written', 'current working directory', 'core dump', 'closed'], 1, 'The process\'s working dir.'], ['Where does lsof get its data?', ['/proc/PID/fd', '/var/log', 'the disk', 'systemd'], 0, 'Magic symlinks.']],
  c: { t: 'Find out which process is listening on port <b>8000</b>.', try: 'lsof -i :8000', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'lsof') && outOf(ctx).includes('8000') },
});
lesson('nice', {
  n: 'nice / renice', k: 'renice', s: 'Starts a process with a lower or higher CPU priority (nice), or changes it for a running process (renice).',
  an: 'Letting others go ahead of you in a queue: the nicer you are (higher number), the more you yield the CPU to others.',
  nm: 'Being <b>nice</b> to other processes.',
  sx: 'nice -n N COMMAND   |   renice -n N -p PID', p: [['nice / renice', 'cmd', 'Set niceness at start / change later.', true], ['-n N', 'opt', 'Niceness -20 (greedy) … 19 (very nice). nice default +10.', true], ['COMMAND | -p PID', 'arg', 'What to run / which process.', true]],
  ex1: 'renice -n 10 -p 777',
  sc: [['setpriority', 'renice: sets the nice value of a process', 'setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 777, 10) = 0'], ['nice → execve', 'nice: adjusts its own niceness, then execs the command (inherited)', 'setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0, 10) = 0; execve(…)']],
  ch: 'The scheduler weight of the process.', out: 'renice: <code>777 (process ID) old priority 0, new priority 10</code>.', ec: '0, 1 on permission denied / no such process.',
  f: [['nice -n N cmd', '', 'Start with niceness N.', 'nice -n 15 tar czf …', true], ['renice -n N -p PID', '', 'Change a running process.', 'renice -n 5 -p 888', true], ['renice N -u USER', '', 'All processes of a user.', 'sudo renice 10 -u alice', false], ['negative N', '', 'Higher priority — root only.', 'sudo renice -n -5 -p 520', true], ['ps -o ni', '', 'Show nice values.', 'ps -eo pid,ni,comm', false]],
  e: [['ps -eo pid,ni,comm', '', 'Current nice values (NI).'], ['renice -n 10 -p 777', '777 (process ID) old priority 0, new priority 10', 'Make the CPU hog nicer.'], ['renice -n -5 -p 888', 'renice: failed to set priority … Permission denied', 'Raising priority needs root.'], ['sudo renice -n -5 -p 888', '', 'Root may.'], ['nice -n 15 sleep 60 &', '', 'Start a background job at low priority.']],
  u: `<p>The scheduler shares CPU time between runnable processes in proportion to their <b>weight</b>, derived from the nice value. Each step is ~10%: a nice-0 process gets about 10× the CPU of a nice-10 one when both compete. If the CPU is idle anyway, nice changes nothing.</p><p>Only <b>root may lower</b> niceness (increase priority) — otherwise everyone would make themselves greedy. Use <code>ionice</code> for disk priority.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>CFS/EEVDF maps nice to weights via <code>sched_prio_to_weight[]</code> (nice 0 = 1024, each step ×1.25). A task's virtual runtime advances inversely to its weight; the task with the smallest vruntime/deadline runs next. Real-time policies (SCHED_FIFO/RR via chrt) bypass nice entirely. RLIMIT_NICE can allow unprivileged users some negative niceness.</p>`,
  mi: [['nice -10 cmd (meaning -10)', 'In nice\'s short syntax, -10 means +10!', 'nice -n -10 cmd (root)'], ['Expecting nice to speed up a lone process', 'Without competition, priority doesn\'t matter.', 'Optimise the program instead'], ['renice 19 sshd', 'Your remote shell becomes sluggish under load.', 'Lower batch jobs, not interactive services']],
  safe: 'Only affects scheduling. (Making critical services very nice can slow them under load.)',
  r: [['top', 'r inside top renices; NI column shows values.'], ['ps', 'ps -o ni.'], ['cgroups', 'cpu.max for hard CPU limits.']],
  cs: ['Nice -20 (greedy) … 19 (polite)', '<code>nice -n 10 cmd</code> start nicer', '<code>renice -n 5 -p PID</code> change', 'Negative values need root', 'Only matters when CPUs are busy'],
  q: [['Which nice value gets the MOST CPU?', ['19', '0', '-20', '10'], 2, 'Lower = higher priority.'], ['Can a normal user set nice -5?', ['Yes', 'No, only root', 'Only for own processes', 'Only with nice not renice'], 1, 'Raising priority needs root.'], ['On an idle machine, nice 19 vs 0 makes…', ['huge difference', 'little/no difference', 'process crash', 'more memory'], 1, 'No competition.']],
  c: { t: 'Make the CPU-hungry process <b>777</b> nicer: set its nice value to <b>10</b>.', try: 'renice -n 10 -p 777', chk: ctx => { const p = procBy(777); return p && p.ni >= 10; } },
});
lesson('signals', {
  n: 'signals', k: 'kill', s: 'Asynchronous notifications the kernel delivers to processes: stop, continue, terminate, reload, user-defined…',
  an: 'Tapping someone on the shoulder with a coloured card: yellow = please wrap up (TERM), red = out now (KILL), blue = pause (STOP), green = carry on (CONT).',
  nm: 'Unix <b>signals</b> (SIGxxx).',
  sx: 'kill -SIGNAL PID   |   kill -l   |   (in scripts) trap \'CMD\' SIGNAL', p: [['kill', 'cmd', 'Send a signal.', true], ['-SIGNAL', 'opt', 'Name (-TERM, -HUP) or number (-15, -1).', false], ['PID', 'arg', 'Target process (or -PGID for a whole group).', true]],
  ex1: 'kill -STOP 888',
  sc: [['kill', 'syscall to deliver the signal', 'kill(888, SIGSTOP) = 0'], ['(kernel)', 'marks the signal pending; when the target next runs, it executes the handler or the default action', ''], ['rt_sigaction', '(in programs) install a handler', 'rt_sigaction(SIGTERM, {sa_handler=0x55…, …}, NULL, 8) = 0']],
  ch: 'Process state: terminated, stopped (T), resumed, or a handler runs (e.g. reload config).', out: 'Usually nothing.', ec: 'kill: 0 if delivered. Killed processes exit with 128+N.',
  f: [['-l', '', 'List all signals.', 'kill -l', true], ['-TERM (15)', '', 'Polite terminate (default).', 'kill PID', true], ['-KILL (9)', '', 'Uncatchable kill.', 'kill -9 PID', true], ['-STOP (19) / -CONT (18)', '', 'Pause / resume (STOP uncatchable).', 'kill -STOP PID', true], ['-HUP (1)', '', 'Hang-up: daemons reload config.', 'sudo kill -HUP 520', false], ['-INT (2)', '', 'Ctrl+C.', '', false], ['-TSTP (20)', '', 'Ctrl+Z.', '', false], ['trap', '', 'Bash: run code when a signal arrives.', "trap 'echo bye' EXIT INT", false]],
  e: [['kill -l', ' 1) SIGHUP …', 'The list.'], ['kill -STOP 888; ps -o pid,stat,comm -p 888', '', 'Paused: state T.'], ['kill -CONT 888', '', 'Resume.'], ['kill 777; kill -9 777', '', 'TERM ignored, KILL works.'], ['sleep 100 & kill -INT %1', '', 'Same as pressing Ctrl+C on it.']],
  u: `<p>Every signal has a <b>default action</b> (terminate, core dump, stop, continue, ignore). A program may install a <b>handler</b> (e.g. web servers reload config on HUP, flush logs on USR1) or <b>ignore</b> a signal — except <b>SIGKILL</b> and <b>SIGSTOP</b>, which the kernel always enforces.</p>${SIG_TABLE}<p>Keyboard: <b>Ctrl+C</b> → SIGINT, <b>Ctrl+Z</b> → SIGTSTP, <b>Ctrl+\\</b> → SIGQUIT, all sent to the foreground process group. In scripts, <code>trap 'cleanup' EXIT INT TERM</code> runs cleanup code (delete temp files) when the script ends or is interrupted.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Per-thread pending masks and per-process shared pending sets live in <code>task_struct->pending</code> / <code>signal->shared_pending</code>; handlers in <code>sighand_struct</code>. Delivery happens on return to user space (<code>do_signal</code>), building a signal frame on the user stack; <code>rt_sigreturn</code> restores state. <code>/proc/PID/status</code> lines SigPnd, SigBlk, SigIgn, SigCgt show the masks in hex. signalfd and sigwaitinfo allow synchronous handling.</p>`,
  mi: [['kill -9 as the first resort', 'No cleanup.', 'TERM first'], ['Expecting kill -STOP to free memory', 'The process keeps its memory.', 'Terminate it if memory is needed'], ['Scripts leaving temp files when Ctrl+C\'d', 'No cleanup handler.', "trap 'rm -f \"$tmp\"' EXIT"]],
  w: 'Sending signals to the wrong PID (or to PID -1, which means “every process you may signal”) can bring down your session or services. <code>kill -9 -1</code> as root kills almost everything.',
  r: [['kill', 'The command that sends signals.'], ['jobs', 'Ctrl+Z / fg / bg use STOP/CONT.'], ['nohup', 'Ignoring SIGHUP.']],
  cs: ['TERM 15 polite · KILL 9 forced · HUP 1 reload', 'STOP 19 / CONT 18 pause/resume', 'KILL and STOP can\'t be caught', 'Ctrl+C INT · Ctrl+Z TSTP', 'Killed by N → exit code 128+N'],
  q: [['Which two signals cannot be caught?', ['TERM & INT', 'KILL & STOP', 'HUP & QUIT', 'USR1 & USR2'], 1, 'Always enforced by the kernel.'], ['What does Ctrl+Z send?', ['SIGINT', 'SIGTSTP', 'SIGKILL', 'SIGHUP'], 1, 'Terminal stop.'], ['A process killed with -9 exits with code…', ['9', '137', '1', '0'], 1, '128 + 9.']],
  c: { t: 'Pause process <b>888</b> with SIGSTOP, then resume it with SIGCONT.', try: 'kill -STOP 888', steps: [['Send SIGSTOP to 888', ctx => ctx.events.some(e => e.type === 'signal' && e.pid === 888 && e.sig === 'STOP')], ['Send SIGCONT to 888', ctx => ctx.events.some(e => e.type === 'signal' && e.pid === 888 && e.sig === 'CONT')]] },
});
lesson('proc', {
  n: '/proc', k: 'cat', s: 'A virtual filesystem where the kernel shows live information about processes and the system as files.',
  an: 'A live dashboard disguised as a filing cabinet: every “file” you open is written by the kernel at the moment you read it.',
  nm: '<b>proc</b>ess filesystem (procfs).',
  sx: 'cat /proc/FILE   |   ls /proc/PID/   |   cat /proc/PID/status', p: [['cat / ls', 'cmd', 'Ordinary tools read it.', true], ['/proc/PID/…', 'arg', 'Per-process info: status, cmdline, environ, fd/, maps, cwd, exe.', false], ['/proc/…', 'arg', 'System info: cpuinfo, meminfo, uptime, loadavg, version, sys/.', false]],
  ex1: 'cat /proc/1200/status',
  sc: [['openat', 'opens a virtual file — no disk involved', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/proc/1200/status", O_RDONLY) = 3'], ['read', 'the kernel GENERATES the text right now from its data structures', 'read(3, "Name:\\tbash\\nState:\\tS (sleeping)…", 131072) = 1350']],
  ch: 'Nothing (writing to /proc/sys/* changes kernel settings — root only).', out: 'Text produced by the kernel.', ec: '0 / 1.',
  f: [['/proc/cpuinfo', '', 'CPU model, cores, flags.', 'cat /proc/cpuinfo', true], ['/proc/meminfo', '', 'Memory details (free uses it).', 'cat /proc/meminfo', true], ['/proc/PID/status', '', 'State, PPID, UIDs, memory of a process.', 'cat /proc/1200/status', true], ['/proc/PID/cmdline', '', 'The command line (NUL-separated).', 'cat /proc/888/cmdline', false], ['/proc/PID/fd/', '', 'Open file descriptors.', 'ls -l /proc/1200/fd', true], ['/proc/self', '', 'Link to the reading process itself.', 'cat /proc/self/status', false], ['/proc/sys/', '', 'Tunable kernel parameters (sysctl).', 'cat /proc/sys/kernel/pid_max', false]],
  e: [['cat /proc/cpuinfo | head -5', 'processor : 0\nvendor_id : GenuineIntel…', 'CPU info.'], ['cat /proc/meminfo | head -3', 'MemTotal: 8041512 kB…', 'Memory.'], ['ls /proc | head', '1\n1200\n2\n345…', 'One directory per PID.'], ['cat /proc/1200/status | head -5', 'Name:\tbash\nState:\tS (sleeping)\nPid:\t1200\nPPid:\t1190…', 'Your shell.'], ['cat /proc/uptime /proc/loadavg', '', 'What uptime reads.'], ['cat /proc/version', 'Linux version 6.8.0-49-generic …', 'Kernel build.']],
  u: `<p>Nothing in /proc lives on disk (<code>ls -l</code> shows size 0) — the kernel generates the content at read time. That's how ps, top, free, uptime and lsof work: they just read /proc. Each running process has a directory named after its PID, with files like <code>status</code>, <code>cmdline</code>, <code>environ</code>, <code>fd/</code> (open files), <code>maps</code> (memory mappings), <code>cwd</code> and <code>exe</code> (symlinks).</p><p><code>/proc/sys</code> exposes tunables (also via <code>sysctl</code>), e.g. <code>/proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward</code>.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>procfs files are implemented with seq_file callbacks (e.g. <code>proc_pid_status</code> reads <code>task_struct</code>/<code>mm_struct</code> fields). Access to sensitive files (environ, mem, fd) is restricted to the owner (ptrace access mode checks); mount option <code>hidepid=2</code> hides other users' processes entirely. Sister filesystem <code>/sys</code> (sysfs) exposes devices and drivers.</p>`,
  mi: [['cp /proc/meminfo backup/ expecting live data later', 'It\'s just a snapshot of that moment.', 'Read /proc when you need the value'], ['echo 1 > /proc/sys/... as user', 'Permission denied.', 'echo 1 | sudo tee /proc/sys/…  (or sysctl -w)'], ['cat /proc/PID/environ of another user', 'Permission denied (contains secrets).', 'sudo if really necessary']],
  safe: 'Reading is harmless; WRITING to /proc/sys changes live kernel settings.',
  r: [['ps', 'Built on /proc.'], ['lsof', 'Reads /proc/PID/fd.'], ['free', 'Reads /proc/meminfo.']],
  cs: ['/proc = live kernel info as files', '/proc/PID/ status, cmdline, fd/, maps', '/proc/cpuinfo · meminfo · uptime · loadavg', '/proc/self = the reading process', '/proc/sys = tunables (sysctl)'],
  q: [['Does /proc use disk space?', ['Yes', 'No, it\'s generated by the kernel on read', 'Only for big files', 'Only /proc/sys'], 1, 'Virtual filesystem.'], ['Where are a process\'s open files listed?', ['/proc/PID/files', '/proc/PID/fd/', '/proc/fd/PID', '/proc/open'], 1, 'fd/.'], ['Which tool is basically a /proc reader?', ['ps', 'cp', 'tar', 'ssh'], 0, 'ps, top, free…']],
  c: { t: 'Read the status of your own shell (PID <b>1200</b>) from /proc.', try: 'cat /proc/1200/status', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'cat') && ctx.line.includes('/proc/') && outOf(ctx).includes('State') },
});
